/-!
# Instantiating the hash algebra - and why the axioms cannot be discharged by a hash

The theorems in `Merkle/Basic.lean` and `Merkle/Proof.lean` are quantified over *every* type
class instance satisfying `Merkle.MerkleHash`. This file shows what it takes to discharge those
assumptions, and it is deliberately honest about the one thing that cannot be done here.

## What is proved

* `Merkle.Examples.Dig` - the **free** Merkle hash algebra on a leaf type `α`: digests are the
  binary trees generated by `leaf` and `node`. Both axioms hold by constructor injectivity, with
  no axioms of any kind. This is the model that makes the security theorems non-vacuous: they
  apply to a concrete, computable algebra whose collision-freeness is a theorem.
* `Merkle.Examples.modThree_notInjective` - a concrete compressing candidate `Fin 4 -> Fin 3`
  together with a *proof that it is not injective*. This is the small formal shadow of the
  general barrier below.

## The barrier, stated plainly

A real deployment wants digests to be *shorter* than the data: `β = Fin 2 ^ 256`, say. But a
collision-free `leaf : α -> β` is injective, so it needs `|α| <= |β|`; and a collision-free
`node : β -> β -> β` is injective on `β × β`, so it needs `|β| ^ 2 <= |β|`. For a finite `β`
with `|β| >= 2` that inequality already fails. So **no finite digest type of bounded size can
satisfy `MerkleHash` for an unbounded leaf type**, and the axioms of `MerkleHash` can only be
*assumed* of a concrete compression function, never proved.

This is not a limitation of the formalization; it is the standard, well-known status of hash
assumptions. What the formalization buys is that the reduction is exact and machine-checked:
every attack on a Merkle root in `Merkle/Proof.lean` is exhibited as a collision in `node` or
`leaf`. A production instantiation would therefore add

    axiom sha256CollFree : CollFree sha256Node

and the rest of the development would apply unchanged. We do not do that here. Instead,
`#print axioms` on every theorem of this library reports only Lean's two standard logical
axioms - `propext` and `Quot.sound` - and never `Classical.choice`, and never `sorryAx`.
-/

namespace Merkle.Examples

/-- The **free** Merkle hash algebra on `α`: the smallest type with an injective `leaf` and an
injective `node`. Digests grow with the tree instead of being compressed, which is exactly what
makes collision-freeness provable rather than assumed. -/
inductive Dig (α : Type) : Type where
  | leaf : α → Dig α
  | node : Dig α → Dig α → Dig α
  deriving DecidableEq

namespace Dig

instance : NodeAlgebra (Dig α) where
  node := Dig.node
  collFree {_a _b _c _d} h := ⟨Dig.node.inj h |>.1, Dig.node.inj h |>.2⟩

instance : MerkleHash α (Dig α) where
  node := Dig.node
  collFree {_a _b _c _d} h := ⟨Dig.node.inj h |>.1, Dig.node.inj h |>.2⟩
  leaf := Dig.leaf
  leafInjective := fun {_x _y} h => Dig.leaf.inj h

end Dig

/-- Sanity check: the free algebra really is a `MerkleHash`, so `rootHash` is available. -/
example : MerkleHash Nat (Dig Nat) := inferInstance

/-- The root digest of a concrete depth-two tree, computed by the kernel. -/
example : rootHash (α := Nat) (β := Dig Nat) (Tree.node (Tree.node (Tree.leaf 1) (Tree.leaf 2))
    (Tree.node (Tree.leaf 3) (Tree.leaf 4))) =
    Dig.node (Dig.node (Dig.leaf 1) (Dig.leaf 2)) (Dig.node (Dig.leaf 3) (Dig.leaf 4)) := rfl

/-! ## Why a compressing hash cannot satisfy the axioms -/

/-- The collision-freeness of `leaf` is exactly injectivity, so it already forces
`|α| <= |β|`. Here is a concrete compressing candidate and a *proof that it fails*: reducing
four values modulo three identifies two of them. -/
def modThree : Fin 4 → Fin 3 := fun i => ⟨i.val % 3, Nat.mod_lt _ (by decide)⟩

theorem modThree_notInjective : ¬ Function.Injective modThree := by
  intro h
  have h₀ : modThree ⟨0, by omega⟩ = modThree ⟨3, by omega⟩ := rfl
  have h₁ : (⟨0, by omega⟩ : Fin 4) = ⟨3, by omega⟩ := h h₀
  have h₂ : (0 : Nat) = 3 := congrArg Fin.val h₁
  omega

/-- The counting fact behind the general barrier, for the smallest non-trivial size: no
function from two elements to one element is injective, because the codomain has only one
element. This is the base case of the pigeonhole argument that rules out every finite
compressing digest type. (The general statement - no injection `Fin (n + 1) → Fin n` - needs a
cardinality theory; we keep this file in pure Lean core, so we record the instance that matters
for the barrier above.) -/
theorem no_injection_two_into_one : ∀ f : Fin 2 → Fin 1, ¬ Function.Injective f := by
  intro f h
  have h₀ : f ⟨0, by omega⟩ = f ⟨1, by omega⟩ := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  have h₁ : (⟨0, by omega⟩ : Fin 2) = ⟨1, by omega⟩ := h h₀
  have h₂ : (0 : Nat) = 1 := congrArg Fin.val h₁
  omega
end Merkle.Examples
